本课程由 Brian Yu 授课。
Brian Yu teaches this course.

6
00:00:51,930 --> 00:00:59,090
Hello, world。这是 CS50，这是由 CS50 的 Brian Yu 主讲的“人工智能与 Python”入门课程。
Hello, world. This is CS50, and this is an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python with CS50's own Brian Yu.

7
00:00:59,440 --> 00:01:06,320
本课程接续 CS50 的内容，探讨现代 AI 基础上的概念和算法。
This course picks up where CS50 itself leaves off and explores the concepts and algorithms at the foundation of modern AI.

8
00:01:06,606 --> 00:01:09,956
我们将首先看看 AI 如何寻找问题的解决方案。
We'll start with a look at how AI can search for solutions to problems.

9
00:01:10,222 --> 00:01:14,820
无论这些问题是学习如何玩游戏，还是试图寻找前往目的地的导航路线。
Whether those problems are learning how to play a game or trying to find driving directions to a destination.

10
00:01:15,246 --> 00:01:23,364
接着，我们将研究 AI 如何表示信息，包括我们确信的知识，以及 AI 可能不确定的一些信息和事件，
We'll then look at how AI can represent information, both knowledge that our AI is certain about, but also information and events about which our AI might be uncertain,

11
00:01:23,600 --> 00:01:29,956
学习如何表达这些信息，更重要的是，学习如何利用这些信息来进行推理并得出新结论。
learning how to represent that information, but more importantly, how to use that information to draw inferences and new conclusions as well.

12
00:01:30,446 --> 00:01:38,340
我们将探讨 AI 如何解决各种类型的优化问题，比如试图最大化利润、最小化成本或满足某些其他约束条件，
We'll explore how AI can solve various types of optimization problems, trying to maximize profits or minimize cost or satisfy some other constraints,

13
00:01:38,606 --> 00:01:44,640
然后转向快速发展的机器学习领域，在那个领域中，我们不会明确告诉 AI 如何解决问题，
before turning our attention to the fast-growing field of machine learning, where we won't tell our AI exactly how to solve a problem,

14
00:01:44,814 --> 00:01:51,524
而是让我们的 AI 获取数据和经验，从而让它能够自主学习如何执行这些任务。
but instead give our AI access to data and experiences so that our AI can learn on its own how to perform these tasks.

15
00:01:52,142 --> 00:01:59,840
特别是，我们将研究神经网络，这是现代机器学习中最流行的工具之一，它的灵感源自人类大脑的学习和推理方式，
In particular, we'll look at neural networks, one of the most popular tools in modern machine learning, inspired by the way that human brains learn and reason as well,

16
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,680
最后，我们将一探自然语言处理的世界，这样不仅是我们人类在学习如何让人工智能开口说话，
before finally taking a look at the world of natural language processing, so that it's not just us humans learning to learn how artificial intelligence is able to speak,

17
00:02:07,854 --> 00:02:11,716
而是 AI 也在学习如何理解和解读人类语言。
but also AI learning how to understand and interpret human language as well.

18
00:02:12,078 --> 00:02:21,600
我们将探索这些思想和算法，并在过程中提供机会让你构建自己的 AI 程序来实现所有这些内容甚至更多。这是 CS50。
We'll explore these ideas and algorithms, and along the way, give you the opportunity to build your own AI programs to implement all of this and more. This is CS50.

19
00:02:44,174 --> 00:02:47,920
好了，欢迎大家来到“人工智能与 Python”入门课程。
All right, welcome, everyone, to an introduction to artificial intelligence with Python.

20
00:02:48,320 --> 00:02:49,180
我的名字是 Brian Yu。
My name is Brian Yu.

21
00:02:49,500 --> 00:02:55,940
在这一课中，我们将探索一些处于人工智能基础上的思想、技术和算法。
And in this class, we'll explore some of the ideas and techniques and algorithms that are at the foundation of artificial intelligence.

22
00:02:56,398 --> 00:02:59,844
现在，人工智能涵盖了非常多类型的技术。
Now, artificial intelligence covers a wide variety of types of techniques.

23
00:03:00,160 --> 00:03:09,840
每当你看到电脑做一些看起来在某种程度上具有智能或理性的事情时，比如在照片中识别出某人的脸，或者能够比人类更好地玩游戏，
Anytime you see a computer do something that appears to be intelligent or rational in some way, like recognizing someone's face in a photo, or being able to play a game better than people can,

24
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:19,588
或者当我们和手机通话时它能理解我们的意思并做出回应。这些都是 AI 或人工智能的例子。
or being able to understand human language when we talk to our phones and they understand what we mean and are able to respond back to us. These are all examples of AI or artificial intelligence.

25
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:24,228
在这一课中，我们将探索让这些 AI 成为可能的某些思想。
And in this class, we'll explore some of the ideas that make that AI possible.

26
00:03:24,622 --> 00:03:33,600
所以我们将从搜索开始，也就是我们有一个 AI，并且我们希望这个 AI 能够为某种问题寻找解决方案，无论那个问题是什么，
So we'll begin our conversations with search, the problem of we have an AI, and we would like the AI to be able to search for solutions to some kind of problem, no matter what that problem might be,

27
00:03:33,760 --> 00:03:43,140
无论是试图获取从 A 点到 B 点的导航路线，还是试图弄清楚如何玩游戏，比如在 tic-tac-toe 游戏中，搞清楚它应该采取什么行动。
whether it's trying to get driving directions from point A to point B, or trying to figure out how to play a game, given a tic-tac-toe game, for example, figuring out what move it ought to make.

28
00:03:43,918 --> 00:03:46,080
在那之后，我们将看看知识。
After that, we'll take a look at knowledge.

29
00:03:46,286 --> 00:03:54,400
理想情况下，我们希望我们的 AI 能够掌握信息，能够表示这些信息，更重要的是，能够从这些信息中进行推理，
Ideally, we want our AI to be able to know information, to be able to represent that information, and more importantly, to be able to draw inferences from that information,

30
00:03:54,638 --> 00:03:58,308
能够利用它已知的信息并得出额外的结论。
to be able to use the information it knows and draw additional conclusions.

31
00:03:58,670 --> 00:04:02,596
所以我们将讨论如何对 AI 进行编程，以实现这一目标。
So we'll talk about how AI can be programmed in order to do just that.

32
00:04:03,118 --> 00:04:11,680
然后我们将探讨不确定性这个话题，讨论如果计算机不确定某个事实，而只是以一定的概率确定时会发生什么。
Then we'll explore the topic of uncertainty, talking about ideas of what happens if a computer isn't sure about a fact, but maybe is only sure with a certain probability.

33
00:04:11,982 --> 00:04:20,480
因此，我们将讨论一些关于概率的理念，以及计算机如何开始处理不确定事件，从而在某种意义上变得更智能
So we'll talk about some of the ideas behind probability and how computers can begin to deal with uncertain events in order to be a little bit more intelligent in that sense

34
00:04:20,685 --> 00:04:20,995
同时也更聪明。
as well.

35
00:04:21,838 --> 00:04:27,908
之后，我们将把注意力转向优化，即当计算机试图为某种目标进行优化的问题。
After that, we'll turn our attention to optimization, problems of when the computer is trying to optimize for some sort of goal.

36
00:04:28,160 --> 00:04:36,720
特别是在可能存在多种解决问题方法的情况下，我们正在寻找更好的方法，或者如果可能的话，是最好的方法。
Especially in a situation where there might be multiple ways that a computer might solve a problem, but we're looking for a better way, or potentially the best way, if that's at all possible.

37
00:04:37,582 --> 00:04:42,464
然后我们将看看机器学习，或者更广泛地说是学习，并探讨当我们拥有数据时，
Then we'll take a look at machine learning, or learning more generally, and looking at how when we have access to data,

38
00:04:42,624 --> 00:04:51,396
我们的计算机如何可以通过从数据和经验中学习来变得非常智能，能够根据更多的数据获取而更好地执行任务。
our computers can be programmed to be quite intelligent by learning from data and learning from experience, being able to perform a task better and better based on greater access to data.

39
00:04:51,694 --> 00:04:57,988
例如，你的电子邮件系统，它能自动识别哪些是重要的邮件，哪些是垃圾邮件。
So, your email, for example, where your email inbox somehow knows which of your emails are good emails and which of your emails are spam.

40
00:04:58,224 --> 00:05:03,748
这些都是计算机能够从过去的经验和过去的数据中学习的例子。
These are all examples of computers being able to learn from past experiences and past data.

41
00:05:04,366 --> 00:05:13,424
我们还将看看计算机如何从人类智能中获取灵感，研究人类大脑的结构以及神经网络如何作为计算机的类比
We'll take a look too at how computers are able to draw inspiration from human intelligence, looking at the structure of the human brain and how neural networks can be a computer analog

42
00:05:13,584 --> 00:05:22,340
来实现这一想法，以及通过利用某种特定类型的程序结构，我们如何编写能够非常高效执行任务的神经网络。
to that sort of idea, and how by taking advantage of a certain type of structure of a computer program, we can write neural networks that are able to perform tasks very, very effectively.

43
00:05:22,926 --> 00:05:28,864
最后，我们将把注意力转向语言，不是编程语言，而是我们每天使用的人类语言。
And then finally, we'll turn our attention to language, not programming languages, but human languages that we speak every day.

44
00:05:29,166 --> 00:05:36,548
并看看当计算机试图理解自然语言时会遇到哪些挑战，以及现代人工智能中的一些
And taking a look at the challenges that come about as a computer tries to understand natural language and how it is that some of the natural language processing that

45
00:05:36,864 --> 00:05:40,100
自然语言处理是如何真正实现的。
occurs in modern artificial intelligence can actually work.

46
00:05:40,974 --> 00:05:43,492
但今天我们将从搜索开始我们的讨论。
But today we'll begin our conversation with search.

47
00:05:43,664 --> 00:05:51,652
这个问题是，当计算机处于某种情况中，或者说智能体处在一个某种环境中时，该如何决定下一步行动，
This problem of trying to figure out what to do when we have some sort of situation that the computer is in, some sort of environment that an agent is in, so to speak,

48
00:05:51,854 --> 00:05:56,388
并且我们希望这个智能体能够设法找到解决该问题的办法。
and we would like for that agent to be able to somehow look for a solution to that problem.

49
00:05:57,070 --> 00:05:59,812
现在，这些问题可以有各种不同的形式。
Now, these problems can come in any number of different types of formats.

50
00:06:00,238 --> 00:06:05,412
例如，一个例子可能是像这种经典的 15 puzzle 滑块拼图，你可能见过类似的。
One example, for instance, might be something like this classic 15 puzzle with the sliding tiles that you might have seen,

51
00:06:05,582 --> 00:06:09,504
其中你试图滑动方块，以确保所有的数字按顺序排列。
where you're trying to slide the tiles in order to make sure that all the numbers line up in order.

52
00:06:09,744 --> 00:06:12,384
这是一个典型的搜索问题的例子。
This is an example of what you might call a search problem.

53
00:06:12,686 --> 00:06:21,060
15 puzzle 从一个初始的混乱状态开始，我们需要某种方法来找到移动步骤，从而让拼图回到解决状态。
The 15 puzzle begins in an initially mixed-up state, and we need some way of finding moves to make in order to return the puzzle to its solved state.

54
00:06:21,614 --> 00:06:23,972
但还有一些类似的问题可以用其他方式来表述。
But there are similar problems that you can frame in other ways.

55
00:06:24,174 --> 00:06:27,812
例如，试图在迷宫中找到出路，是另一个搜索问题的例子。
Trying to find your way through a maze, for example, is another example of a search problem.

56
00:06:28,144 --> 00:06:36,740
你从一个地方开始，有一个想要到达的目标，并且你需要找出正确的动作序列，将你从初始状态带到目标。
You begin in one place, you have some goal of where you're trying to get to, and you need to figure out the correct sequence of actions that will take you from that initial state to the goal.

57
00:06:37,390 --> 00:06:45,540
虽然这有点抽象，但每当我们在这门课中讨论解决迷宫问题时，你都可以把它转化成更贴近现实的东西，比如导航路线。
And while this is a little bit abstract, anytime we talk about maze solving in this class, you can translate it to something a little more real world, something like driving directions.

58
00:06:45,774 --> 00:06:54,624
如果你曾好奇 Google Maps 是如何判断从 A 点到 B 点的最佳路径，以及根据交通状况在什么时间该转弯，
If you ever wonder how Google Maps is able to figure out what is the best way for you to get from point A to point B and what turns to make at what time, depending on traffic, for example,

59
00:06:54,784 --> 00:06:56,996
它通常就是某种搜索算法。
it's often some sort of search algorithm.

60
00:06:57,264 --> 00:07:03,652
你有一个 AI，它试图通过执行一系列动作，从初始位置到达某个目标。
You have an AI that is trying to get from an initial position to some sort of goal by taking some sequence of actions.

61
00:07:04,110 --> 00:07:13,860
所以我们今天的讨论将从思考这类搜索问题开始，以及为了让 AI 能找到好的解决方案，解决这类搜索问题需要包含哪些要素。
So we'll start our conversations today by thinking about these types of search problems and what goes in to solving a search problem like this in order for an AI to be able to find a good solution.

62
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:19,268
不过为了做到这一点，我们需要引入一些术语，其中有些我已经用过了。
In order to do so though, we're going to need to introduce a little bit of terminology, some of which I've already used.

63
00:07:19,584 --> 00:07:22,212
但我们要考虑的第一个术语是智能体。
But the first term we'll need to think about is an agent.

64
00:07:22,606 --> 00:07:30,340
智能体就是一个能够感知其环境的实体，它能以某种方式感知周围的事物并对环境做出相应的反应。
An agent is just some entity that perceives its environment, it somehow is able to perceive the things around it and act on that environment in some way.

65
00:07:30,702 --> 00:07:38,464
在导航路线的例子中，你的智能体可能是一个汽车的抽象模型，它试图找出采取哪些动作才能到达目的地。
So in the case of the driving directions, your agent might be some representation of a car that is trying to figure out what actions to take in order to arrive at a destination.

66
00:07:38,704 --> 00:07:47,104
在带滑动瓷片的 15 puzzle 中，智能体可能是试图解决那个谜题、试图弄清楚该移动哪些瓷片的 AI 或人
In the case of the 15 puzzle with the sliding tiles, the agent might be the AI or the person that is trying to solve that puzzle, to try and figure out what tiles to move

67
00:07:47,278 --> 00:07:49,060
以便找到解决方案。
in order to get to that solution.

68
00:07:49,966 --> 00:07:52,100
接下来，我们引入了状态的概念。
Next, we introduced the idea of a state.

69
00:07:52,654 --> 00:07:56,612
状态就是智能体在环境中的某种配置。
A state is just some configuration of the agent in its environment.

70
00:07:57,102 --> 00:08:01,316
例如在 15 puzzle 中，任何一个状态都可能对应这三个状态中的其中一个。
So in the 15 puzzle, for example, any state might be any one of these three, for example.

71
00:08:01,584 --> 00:08:08,612
状态只是瓷片的某种排列，每一个状态都不同，并且需要略有不同的解决方案。
A state is just some configuration of the tiles, and each of these states is different and is going to require a slightly different solution.

72
00:08:08,784 --> 00:08:16,676
为了从这个初始状态到达目标（也就是我们想要达到的地方），在每一个状态中都需要一系列不同的动作。
A different sequence of actions will be needed in each one of these in order to get from this initial state to the goal, which is where we're trying to get.

73
00:08:17,422 --> 00:08:18,384
那么接下来是初始状态。
So the initial state then.

74
00:08:18,464 --> 00:08:18,944
那是什么？
What is that?

75
00:08:19,214 --> 00:08:21,984
初始状态就是智能体开始时的那个状态。
The initial state is just the state where the agent begins.

76
00:08:22,144 --> 00:08:24,612
它是我们开始时的其中一个状态。
It is one such state where we're going to start from.

77
00:08:24,846 --> 00:08:28,420
可以说，这是我们搜索算法的起点。
And this is going to be the starting point for our search algorithm, so to speak.

78
00:08:28,686 --> 00:08:35,332
我们将从这个初始状态开始，并对其进行推理，思考我们可以对该初始状态采取哪些动作
We're going to begin with this initial state and then start to reason about it, to think about what actions might we apply to that initial state

79
00:08:35,662 --> 00:08:41,904
来弄清楚如何从起点到达终点，即从初始位置到我们的目标。
in order to figure out how to get from the beginning to the end, from the initial position to whatever our goal happens to be.

80
00:08:42,830 --> 00:08:45,456
那我们如何从那个初始位置走到目标呢？
And how do we make our way from that initial position to the goal?

81
00:08:45,678 --> 00:08:47,748
嗯，最终是通过执行动作来实现的。
Well, ultimately, it's via taking actions.

82
00:08:48,078 --> 00:08:51,044
动作就是我们在任何给定状态下可以做出的选择。
Actions are just choices that we can make in any given state.

83
00:08:51,534 --> 00:08:59,216
在 AI 中，我们总是试图更精确地形式化这些概念，以便我们可以用更数学化的方式来对它们进行编程。
And in AI, we're always going to try to formalize these ideas a little bit more precisely, such that we could program them a little bit more mathematically, so to speak.

84
00:08:59,502 --> 00:09:00,772
这将是一个反复出现的主题。
So this will be a recurring theme.

85
00:09:01,006 --> 00:09:04,196
我们可以更精确地将动作定义为一个函数。
And we can more precisely define actions as a function.

86
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:13,188
我们将定义一个名为 动作 的函数，它接受输入 S，其中 S 是存在于我们环境中的某个状态。
We're going to effectively define a function called actions that takes an input S, where S is going to be some state that exists inside of our environment.

87
00:09:13,550 --> 00:09:22,020
动作(S) 将这个状态作为输入，并输出在该状态下可以执行的所有动作的集合。
And actions of S is going to take this state as input and return as output the set of all actions that can be executed in that state.

88
00:09:22,510 --> 00:09:27,332
因此，某些动作可能只在特定的状态下有效，而在其他状态下无效。
And so it's possible that some actions are only valid in certain states and not in other states.

89
00:09:27,630 --> 00:09:29,572
我们很快就会看到相关的例子。
And we'll see examples of that soon too.

90
00:09:30,382 --> 00:09:36,496
例如在 15 puzzle 中，大部分时间通常有四个可选的动作。
So in the case of the 15 puzzle, for example, there are generally going to be four possible actions that we can do most of the time.

91
00:09:36,750 --> 00:09:42,016
例如，我们可以向右滑动一个方块，向左滑动、向上滑动或者向下滑动。
We can slide a tile to the right, slide a tile to the left, slide a tile up, or slide a tile down, for example.

92
00:09:42,254 --> 00:09:45,216
这些就是我们可以采取的动作。
And those are going to be the actions that are available to us.

93
00:09:45,870 --> 00:09:53,700
因此，我们的 AI，也就是我们的程序，需要对状态进行编码，通常是某种数值格式，还需要对这些动作进行编码，
So somehow our AI, our program, needs some encoding of the state, which is often going to be in some numerical format, and some encoding of these actions,

94
00:09:54,094 --> 00:09:56,868
但它还需要对这些事物之间的关系进行编码。
but it also needs some encoding of the relationship between these things.

95
00:09:57,136 --> 00:10:00,036
状态和动作之间是如何关联的？
How do the states and actions relate to one another?

96
00:10:00,590 --> 00:10:10,692
为了实现这一点，我们将引入一个状态转移模型，它描述了在某个状态下执行某个可用动作后，我们得到了什么状态。
And in order to do that, we'll introduce to our AI a transition model, which will be a description of what state we get after we perform some available action in some other state.

97
00:10:11,278 --> 00:10:17,284
同样，我们可以更精确一点，用一种更正式的方式来定义这个状态转移模型，也就是作为一个函数。
And again, we can be a little bit more precise about this, define this transition model a little bit more formally, again, as a function.

98
00:10:17,742 --> 00:10:21,636
这个函数将被称为 result 函数，这次它有两个输入。
The function is going to be a function called result that this time takes two inputs.

99
00:10:22,158 --> 00:10:27,652
第一个输入是 S，即某个状态；第二个输入是 A，即某个动作。
Input number one is S, some state, and input number two is A, some action.

100
00:10:28,238 --> 00:10:35,908
这个 result 函数的输出，就是我们在状态 S 中执行动作 A 后得到的状态。
And the output of this function result is it is going to give us the state that we get after we perform action A in state S.

101
00:10:36,942 --> 00:10:40,176
让我们看一个例子，来更精确地了解这到底意味着什么。
So let's take a look at an example to see more precisely what this actually means.

102
00:10:40,494 --> 00:10:43,616
这里是 15 puzzle 的一个状态示例。
Here's an example of a state of the 15 puzzle, for example.

103
00:10:43,886 --> 00:10:46,884
而这里是一个动作的例子：将一个方块向右滑动。
And here's an example of an action, sliding a tile to the right.

104
00:10:47,470 --> 00:10:50,436
如果我们把这些作为输入传给 result 函数会发生什么？
What happens if we pass these as inputs to the result function?

105
00:10:50,734 --> 00:10:57,412
同样，result 函数将这个棋盘，也就是这个状态，作为第一个输入，并将一个动作作为第二个输入。
Again, the result function takes this board, this state, as its first input, and it takes an action as a second input.

106
00:10:57,678 --> 00:11:02,656
当然，这里我用视觉化的方式来描述，这样你们就能直观地看到什么是状态，什么是动作。
And of course, here I'm describing things visually so that you can see visually what the state is and what the action is.

107
00:11:02,990 --> 00:11:10,976
在计算机中，你可能会将其中一个动作表示为一个代表该动作的数字，或者如果你熟悉枚举类型（enums），它允许你列举出多种可能性，
In a computer, you might represent one of these actions as just some number that represents the action, or if you're familiar with enums that allow you to enumerate multiple possibilities,

108
00:11:11,056 --> 00:11:12,176
它可能就是那样的。
it might be something like that.

109
00:11:12,430 --> 00:11:18,096
而这个状态可能仅仅被表示为一个包含所有这些数字的一维或二维数组。
And this state might just be represented as an array or two-dimensional array of all of these numbers that exist.

110
00:11:18,318 --> 00:11:20,612
但在这里，我们用视觉化的方式来展示，方便你们观察。
But here we're going to show it visually just so you can see it.

111
00:11:21,454 --> 00:11:26,948
但是当我们把这个状态和这个动作输入到 result 函数时，输出是一个新状态。
But when we take this state and this action, pass it into the result function, the output is a new state.

112
00:11:27,310 --> 00:11:32,004
这就是我们拿起一个方块并向右滑动后得到的状态，这也是我们得到的结果。
The state we get after we take a tile and slide it to the right, and this is the state we get as a result.

113
00:11:32,462 --> 00:11:38,980
如果我们有不同的动作和不同的状态，比如把它们传给 result 函数，我们就会得到完全不同的结果。
If we had a different action and a different state, for example, and pass that into the result function, we'd get a different answer altogether.

114
00:11:39,470 --> 00:11:45,796
因此，result 函数需要负责处理如何将一个状态和一个动作结合起来并得出结果，
So the result function needs to take care of figuring out how to take a state and take an action and get what results,

115
00:11:46,096 --> 00:11:52,452
这就是我们的状态转移模型，它描述了状态和动作是如何相互关联的。
and this is going to be our transition model that describes how it is that states and actions are related to each other.

116
00:11:53,326 --> 00:12:00,420
如果我们把这个状态转移模型放到更宏观的视角下，去思考整个问题，我们就可以形成所谓的“状态空间”，
If we take this transition model and think about it more generally and across the entire problem, we can form what we might call a state space,

117
00:12:00,846 --> 00:12:09,156
即通过执行零个、一个、两个或更多动作的任何序列，从初始状态可以到达的所有状态的集合。
the set of all of the states we can get from the initial state via any sequence of actions, by taking zero or one or two or more actions in addition to that.

118
00:12:09,518 --> 00:12:15,044
因此我们可以画出一个类似这样的图，其中每个状态都由一个游戏棋盘来表示。
So we could draw a diagram that looks something like this, where every state is represented here by a game board.

119
00:12:15,598 --> 00:12:20,484
并且有箭头连接每一个状态到从该状态可以到达的所有其他状态。
And there are arrows that connect every state to every other state we can get to from that state.

120
00:12:20,718 --> 00:12:23,616
而这个状态空间比你在这里看到的要大得多。
And the state space is much larger than what you see just here.

121
00:12:23,790 --> 00:12:27,332
这只是状态空间实际可能呈现出的样子的一个示例。
This is just a sample of what the state space might actually look like.

122
00:12:28,110 --> 00:12:36,260
通常情况下，在许多搜索问题中，无论是这个特定的 15 puzzle，还是驾驶路线，或者是其他什么，状态空间看起来都会像这样。
And in general, across many search problems, whether they're this particular 15 puzzle or driving directions or something else, the state space is going to look something like this.

123
00:12:36,590 --> 00:12:40,388
我们有独立的状态，以及连接它们的箭头。
We have individual states and arrows that are connecting them.

124
00:12:40,974 --> 00:12:48,292
而且为了简化，我们通常会将这整个系统的表述简化为图，即一系列的节点
And oftentimes, just for simplicity, we'll simplify our representation of this entire thing as a graph, some sequence of nodes

125
00:12:48,942 --> 00:12:50,336
和连接节点的边。
and edges that connect nodes.

126
00:12:50,574 --> 00:12:54,336
但你可以把这种更抽象的表达方式看作是完全相同的概念。
But you can think of this more abstract representation as the exact same idea.

127
00:12:54,638 --> 00:12:59,616
每一个小圆圈或节点都代表我们问题中的其中一个状态。
Each of these little circles or nodes is going to represent one of the states inside of our problem.

128
00:12:59,854 --> 00:13:07,972
这里的箭头代表我们在任何特定状态下可以采取的动作，带我们从一个特定状态进入另一个状态，
And the arrows here represent the actions that we can take in any particular state, taking us from one particular state to another state,

129
00:13:08,302 --> 00:13:08,644
例如。
for example.

130
00:13:10,220 --> 00:13:10,480
好的。
All right.

131
00:13:10,798 --> 00:13:16,576
所以现在我们有了这个概念：节点代表这些状态，动作能带我们从一个状态到另一个状态，
so now we have this idea of nodes that are representing these states, actions that can take us from one state to another,

132
00:13:16,750 --> 00:13:20,836
以及定义在执行特定动作后会发生什么的状态转移模型。
and a transition model that defines what happens after we take a particular action.

133
00:13:21,358 --> 00:13:26,256
所以下一步我们需要弄清楚的是，我们如何知道 AI 何时完成了问题的求解。
So the next step we need to figure out is how we know when the AI is done solving the problem.

134
00:13:26,574 --> 00:13:30,404
AI 需要某种方式来判断它何时到达了目标，即发现目标。
The AI needs some way to know when it gets to the goal that it's found the goal.

135
00:13:30,926 --> 00:13:34,884
所以接下来我们需要在我们的人工智能中加入一个目标测试。
So the next thing we'll need to encode into our artificial intelligence is a goal test.

136
00:13:35,470 --> 00:13:38,596
一种确定给定状态是否为目标状态的方法。
Some way to determine whether a given state is a goal state.

137
00:13:39,342 --> 00:13:42,496
在像驾驶路线这样的情况下，这可能非常简单。
In the case of something like driving directions, it might be pretty easy.

138
00:13:42,702 --> 00:13:48,964
如果你处于一个对应用户输入的目标地点的状态，那么你就知道你处于目标状态了。
If you're in a state that corresponds to whatever the user typed in as their intended destination, well then you know you're in a goal state.

139
00:13:49,166 --> 00:13:52,816
在 15 puzzle 中，可能是检查数字是否全部按升序排列。
In the 15 puzzle, it might be checking the numbers to make sure they're all in ascending order.

140
00:13:53,038 --> 00:13:57,924
但 AI 需要某种方式来编码它当前所处的任何状态是否是一个目标。
But the AI needs some way to encode whether or not any state they happen to be in is a goal.

141
00:13:58,286 --> 00:14:04,096
有些问题可能只有一个目标，比如迷宫，你有一个初始位置和一个终点位置，那个就是目标。
And some problems might have one goal, like a maze where you have one initial position and one ending position, and that's the goal.

142
00:14:04,366 --> 00:14:13,616
在其他更复杂的问题中，你可以设想存在多个可能的目标，有多种解决问题的方法，我们可能并不在意电脑找到哪一个
In other, more complex problems, you might imagine that there are multiple possible goals, that there are multiple ways to solve a problem, and we might not care which one the computer finds

143
00:14:13,776 --> 00:14:16,260
只要它能找到某个特定的目标就行。
as long as it does find a particular goal.

144
00:14:17,326 --> 00:14:23,588
然而，有时计算机不仅关心找到目标，还关心找到一个好的、或者说成本较低的。
However, sometimes a computer doesn't just care about finding a goal, but finding a goal well, or one with a low cost.

145
00:14:24,046 --> 00:14:30,116
这就是为什么我们用来定义这些搜索问题的最后一个术语是所谓的路径代价。
And it's for that reason that the last piece of terminology that we'll use to define these search problems is something called a path cost.

146
00:14:30,670 --> 00:14:37,376
你可以想象在驾驶路线的情况下，如果我说我要从 A 点到 B 点的路线，
You might imagine that in the case of driving directions, it would be pretty annoying if I said I wanted directions from point A to point B,

147
00:14:37,582 --> 00:14:45,092
而 Google Maps 给我的却是包含很多不必要绕路的长路线，导致到达目的地的时间比预期要长。
and the route the Google Maps gave me was a long route with lots of detours that were unnecessary that took longer than it should have for me to get to that destination.

148
00:14:45,518 --> 00:14:51,620
正因如此，在构建搜索问题时，我们通常会给每条路径赋予某种数值成本，
And it's for that reason that when we're formulating search problems, we'll often give every path some sort of numerical cost,

149
00:14:52,110 --> 00:15:00,336
一个告诉我们采取这个特定选项成本是多少的数字，然后告诉我们的 AI，与其只是寻找一个解决方案，
some number telling us how expensive it is to take this particular option, and then tell our AI that instead of just finding a solution,

150
00:15:00,558 --> 00:15:11,748
即从初始状态到目标的一种方式，我们更希望找到一个能最小化路径代价的方案，也就是成本更低、耗时更短或最小化某些其他数值。
some way of getting from the initial state to the goal, we'd really like to find one that minimizes this path cost, that is less expensive or takes less time or minimizes some other numerical value.

151
00:15:12,494 --> 00:15:21,188
如果我们再次查看这个图，我们可以从图形上来表示这一点。想象一下这些箭头中的每一个，也就是我们从一个状态到另一个状态所能采取的每一个动作，
We can represent this graphically if we take a look at this graph again, and imagine that each of these arrows, each of these actions that we can take from one state to another state,

152
00:15:21,550 --> 00:15:23,524
都有一个数值与之相关。
has some sort of number associated with it.

153
00:15:23,776 --> 00:15:32,336
这个数字就是该特定动作的路径代价，其中某些动作的成本可能比其他动作更高，
That number being the path cost of this particular action, where some of the costs for any particular action might be more expensive than the cost for some other action,

154
00:15:32,496 --> 00:15:33,060
例如。
for example.

155
00:15:33,518 --> 00:15:35,716
虽然这种情况只会在某些类型的问题中出现。
Although this will only happen in some sorts of problems.

156
00:15:36,110 --> 00:15:42,308
在其他问题中，我们可以简化图表，并假设任何特定动作的成本都是一样的。
In other problems, we can simplify the diagram and just assume that the cost of any particular action is the same.

157
00:15:42,606 --> 00:15:49,764
这可能就是 15 puzzle 之类的情况，在这种情况下，向右移动还是向左移动并没有区别。
And this is probably the case in something like the 15 puzzle, for example, where it doesn't really make a difference whether I'm moving right or moving left.

158
00:15:49,936 --> 00:15:58,736
唯一重要的是从 A 点到 B 点所需要采取的总步数，而且每一步的成本都是相等的。
The only thing that matters is the total number of steps that I have to take to get from point A to point B, and each of those steps is of equal cost.

159
00:15:58,896 --> 00:16:02,084
我们可以简单地假设它的成本是某个常数，比如 1。
We can just assume it's of some constant cost like one.

160
00:16:03,246 --> 00:16:07,428
因此，这构成了我们所认为的搜索问题的基础。
And so this now forms the basis for what we might consider to be a search problem.

161
00:16:07,696 --> 00:16:15,812
一个搜索问题包含某种初始状态，即我们的起点；一些我们可以采取的动作，或者在任何给定状态下可以采取的多个动作；
A search problem has some sort of initial state, some place where we begin, some sort of action that we can take, or multiple actions that we can take in any given state,

162
00:16:16,270 --> 00:16:24,548
并且它有一个状态转移模型，也就是一种定义当我们从一个状态出发并执行一个动作时会发生什么的机制，结果我们到达了哪个状态？
and it has a transition model, some way of defining what happens when we go from one state and take one action, what state do we end up with as a result?

163
00:16:25,134 --> 00:16:29,376
除此之外，我们还需要一些目标测试来判断我们是否达到了目标。
In addition to that, we need some goal test to know whether or not we've reached a goal.

164
00:16:29,614 --> 00:16:37,536
然后我们需要一个路径代价函数，它告诉我们对于任何特定的路径，通过遵循一系列动作，该路径的成本是多少。
And then we need a path cost function that tells us for any particular path, by following some sequence of actions, how expensive is that path.

165
00:16:37,696 --> 00:16:43,556
从金钱、时间或我们试图最小化使用的其他资源的角度来看，它的成本是多少？
What is its cost in terms of money or time or some other resource that we are trying to minimize our usage of?

166
00:16:44,238 --> 00:16:46,736
而最终的目标是找到一个解。
And the goal ultimately is to find a solution.

167
00:16:47,054 --> 00:16:52,932
在这种情况下，解仅仅是让我们从初始状态到达目标状态的一系列动作。
Where a solution in this case is just some sequence of actions that will take us from the initial state to the goal state.

168
00:16:53,166 --> 00:17:02,596
理想情况下，我们不仅想要任何一个解，而是最优解，即在所有可能解中路径代价最低的那个解。
And ideally, we'd like to find not just any solution, but the optimal solution, which is a solution that has the lowest path cost among all of the possible solutions.

169
00:17:02,926 --> 00:17:10,976
在某些情况下，可能会有多个最优解，但最优解意味着在寻找该解的过程中，没有任何办法能做得更好。
And in some cases there might be multiple optimal solutions, but an optimal solution just means that there is no way that we could have done better in terms of finding that solution.

170
00:17:12,366 --> 00:17:13,666
现在我们已经定义了问题。
So now we've defined the problem.

171
00:17:13,868 --> 00:17:18,500
现在我们需要开始思考如何解决这类搜索问题。
And now we need to begin to figure out how it is that we're going to solve this kind of search problem.

172
00:17:18,925 --> 00:17:26,016
为了做到这一点，你可能会想到我们的计算机需要表示关于这个特定问题的大量数据。
And in order to do so, you'll probably imagine that our computer is going to need to represent a whole bunch of data about this particular problem.

173
00:17:26,175 --> 00:17:31,940
我们需要表示我们在问题中所处位置的数据，并且我们可能需要同时考虑多个不同的选项。
We need to represent data about where we are in the problem, and we might need to be considering multiple different options at once.

174
00:17:32,462 --> 00:17:40,324
通常当我们试图将与状态相关的一堆数据打包在一起时，我们会使用一种被称为“节点”的数据结构。
And oftentimes when we're trying to package a whole bunch of data related to a state together, we'll do so using a data structure that we're going to call a node.

175
00:17:40,718 --> 00:17:44,896
节点是一个仅用于追踪各种不同数值的数据结构。
A node is a data structure that is just going to keep track of a variety of different values.

176
00:17:45,070 --> 00:17:50,116
特别是在搜索问题的情况下，它将专门记录这四个值。
And specifically in the case of a search problem, it's going to keep track of these four values in particular.

177
00:17:50,606 --> 00:17:54,052
每个节点都会记录一个状态，即我们当前所处的状态。
Every node is going to keep track of a state, the state we're currently on.

178
00:17:54,542 --> 00:17:57,060
而且每个节点还会记录一个父节点。
And every node is also going to keep track of a parent.

179
00:17:57,518 --> 00:18:03,108
父节点就是我们之前的状态，或者说为了到达当前状态而使用的那个节点。
A parent being the state before us, or the node that we used in order to get to this current state.

180
00:18:03,598 --> 00:18:12,708
这非常重要，因为最终当我们到达目标节点、也就是到达终点时，我们想知道为了到达那个目标所使用的一系列动作。
And this is going to be relevant because eventually, once we reach the goal node, once we get to the end, we want to know what sequence of actions we use in order to get to that goal.

181
00:18:13,070 --> 00:18:21,376
我们通过查看这些父节点来获知信息，从而追踪到目标、到当前状态以及到上一个状态的路径，
And the way we'll know that is by looking at these parents to keep track of what led us to the goal and what led us to that state and what led us to the state before that,

182
00:18:21,646 --> 00:18:29,700
以此类推，回溯到起点，这样我们就知道从头到尾所需的完整动作序列了。
so on and so forth, backtracking our way to the beginning so that we know the entire sequence of actions we needed in order to get from the beginning to the end.

183
00:18:30,734 --> 00:18:35,748
节点还会记录我们采取了什么动作，从而从父节点到达当前状态。
The node is also going to keep track of what action we took in order to get from the parent to the current state.

184
00:18:36,078 --> 00:18:39,108
而且，节点还会记录路径代价。
And the node is also going to keep track of a path cost.

185
00:18:39,566 --> 00:18:47,856
换句话说，它会记录一个数值，代表从初始状态到我们当前所在状态所花费的时间。
In other words, it's going to keep track of the number that represents how long it took to get from the initial state to the state that we currently happen to be at.

186
00:18:48,142 --> 00:18:54,276
当我们开始讨论更广泛的搜索问题中可以做的一些优化时，就会看到这其中的重要性。
And we'll see why this is relevant as we start to talk about some of the optimizations that we can make in terms of these search problems more generally.

187
00:18:55,566 --> 00:18:58,576
这就是我们将用来解决问题的数据结构。
So this is the data structure that we're going to use in order to solve the problem.

188
00:18:58,862 --> 00:19:00,336
现在让我们来谈谈方法。
And now let's talk about the approach.

189
00:19:00,590 --> 00:19:03,428
我们究竟该如何开始解决这个问题呢？
How might we actually begin to solve the problem?

190
00:19:03,950 --> 00:19:10,336
正如你们所想的，我们要做的就是从一个特定的状态开始，并从那里开始探索。
Well, as you might imagine, what we're going to do is we're going to start at one particular state and we're just going to explore from there.

191
00:19:10,702 --> 00:19:16,260
直观的逻辑是，从给定的状态出发，我们有多个可选路径，我们将去探索这些选项。
The intuition is that from a given state, we have multiple options that we could take, and we're going to explore those options.

192
00:19:16,782 --> 00:19:21,828
一旦我们探索了这些选项，就会发现还有更多的选项变得可用。
And once we explore those options, we'll find that more options than that are going to make themselves available.

193
00:19:22,286 --> 00:19:28,868
我们将把所有可用的选项存储在一个名为「前沿」的单一数据结构中。
And we're going to consider all of the available options to be stored inside of a single data structure that we'll call the frontier.

194
00:19:29,294 --> 00:19:36,036
前沿代表所有我们可能接下来去探索、但尚未探索或访问过的东西。
