5204
08:10:29,806 --> 08:10:39,492
因为这个决策边界确实把所有的红点和蓝点分开了，因为红点落在这个决策边界的这一侧，
Like this decision boundary does separate all of the red points from all of the blue points, because the red points fall on this side of this decision boundary,

5205
08:10:39,664 --> 08:10:42,180
而蓝点落在决策边界的另一侧。
the blue points fall on the other side of the decision boundary.

5206
08:10:42,638 --> 08:10:47,268
但我们可能会认为，这并不是一个更好的预测。
But this we would probably argue is not as good of a prediction.

5207
08:10:47,504 --> 08:10:55,012
尽管根据我们用于训练这个机器学习模型的所有可用训练数据来看，它似乎更准确，
Even though it seems to be more accurate based on all of the available training data that we have for training this machine learning model,

5208
08:10:55,470 --> 08:10:58,064
但我们可能会说，它可能无法很好地泛化。
we might say that it's probably not going to generalize well.

5209
08:10:58,144 --> 08:11:06,084
也就是说，如果还有像这里和那里的其他数据点，我们仍可能认为它们是下雨天，因为我们认为这只是一个离群值。
That if there were other data points like here and there, we might still want to consider those to be rainy days, because we think this was probably just an outlier.

5210
08:11:06,606 --> 08:11:13,028
所以如果你唯一在乎的是最小化你现有数据的损失，你就面临着过拟合的风险。
So if the only thing you care about is minimizing the loss on the data you have available to you, you run the risk of overfitting.

5211
08:11:13,230 --> 08:11:15,108
这种情况也会发生在分类案例中。
And this can happen in the classification case.

5212
08:11:15,438 --> 08:11:18,052
它也可能发生在回归案例中。
It can also happen in the regression case.

5213
08:11:18,382 --> 08:11:26,692
在这里，我们预测了一条关系到广告和销售的相当不错的线，试图预测给定广告量下的销售额。
That here we predicted what we thought was a pretty good line relating advertising to sales, trying to predict what sales were going to be for a given amount of advertising.

5214
08:11:26,926 --> 08:11:35,268
但我可以画出一条能更好地预测训练数据的线，它看起来就像这样，把所有不同的数据点都连起来，
But I could come up with a line that does a better job of predicting the training data, and it would be something that looks like this, just connecting all of the various different data points,

5215
08:11:35,584 --> 08:11:37,504
这样一来就完全没有损失了。
and now there is no loss at all.

5216
08:11:37,664 --> 08:11:44,708
现在我已经完美地预测出在任何广告量下销售额是多少，而且对于我掌握的所有数据来说，它都是准确的。
Now I've perfectly predicted, given any advertising, what sales are, and for all the data available to me, it's going to be accurate.

5217
08:11:45,294 --> 08:11:47,824
但它可能无法很好地泛化。
But it's probably not going to generalize very well.

5218
08:11:47,984 --> 08:11:52,260
我在可用的训练数据上让模型过拟合了。
I have overfit my model on the training data that is available to me.

5219
08:11:52,878 --> 08:11:59,716
因此，通常情况下，我们想要避免过拟合。我们需要一些策略来确保我们的模型没有针对特定数据集过拟合。
And so in general, we want to avoid overfitting. We'd like strategies to make sure that we haven't overfit our model to a particular data set.

5220
08:12:00,078 --> 08:12:02,052
有几种方法可以尝试这样做。
And there are a number of ways that you could try to do this.

5221
08:12:02,638 --> 08:12:05,476
一种方法是去审视我们究竟在优化什么。
One way is by examining what it is that we're optimizing for.

5222
08:12:05,710 --> 08:12:11,620
在一个优化问题中，我们所做的只是声明：这里存在某种代价，而我想要最小化这个代价。
In an optimization problem, all we do is we say, you know, there is some cost, and I want to minimize that cost.

5223
08:12:12,462 --> 08:12:21,092
到目前为止，我们将代价函数——即假设的成本——定义为该假设的经验损失。
And so far, we've defined that cost function, the cost of a hypothesis, just as being equal to the empirical loss of that hypothesis.

5224
08:12:21,184 --> 08:12:28,996
就像是实际数据点（即输出）与基于该特定假设所预测出的结果之间的差距。
Like how far away are the actual data points, the outputs, away from what I predicted them to be based on that particular hypothesis.

5225
08:12:29,358 --> 08:12:36,676
如果你只是试图最小化代价，也就是在这种情况下最小化损失，那么结果可能是模型过拟合。
And if all you're trying to do is minimize cost, meaning minimizing the loss in this case, then the result is going to be that you might overfit.

5226
08:12:37,024 --> 08:12:47,300
为了最小化成本，你可能会试图找到一种方法来完美匹配所有输入数据，而这可能导致在特定输入数据上产生过拟合。
That to minimize cost, you're going to try and find a way to perfectly match all the input data, and that might happen as a result of overfitting on that particular input data.

5227
08:12:48,494 --> 08:12:52,228
因此，为了解决这个问题，你可以给代价函数增加一些内容。
So in order to address this, you could add something to the cost function.

5228
08:12:52,558 --> 08:12:59,332
什么才算代价？不仅是损失，还要包含衡量假设复杂程度的某些指标。
What counts as cost Well, not just loss, but also some measure of the complexity of the hypothesis.

5229
08:12:59,534 --> 08:13:05,984
假设的复杂度是你需要定义的一部分，比如我们的这条线看起来有多复杂？
The complexity of the hypothesis is something that you would need to define for, how complicated does our line look?

5230
08:13:06,144 --> 08:13:15,044
这有点像“奥卡姆剃刀”式的方法，即我们倾向于选择更简单的决策边界，比如直线或者一些较简单的曲线。
This is sort of an Occam's razor style approach where we want to give preference to a simpler decision boundary, like a straight line, for example, some simpler curve,

5231
08:13:15,264 --> 08:13:21,264
而不是那些虽然能更好地拟合训练数据、但可能无法很好泛化的复杂结构。
as opposed to something far more complex that might represent the training data better, but might not generalize as well.

5232
08:13:21,424 --> 08:13:30,148
我们通常认为更简单的解决方案往往是更好的，而且更有可能在其他输入上良好泛化。
We'll generally say that a simpler solution is probably the better solution and probably the one that is more likely to generalize well to other inputs.

5233
08:13:31,182 --> 08:13:38,372
所以我们不仅衡量损失，还衡量复杂度，现在这两者都会被纳入考虑，来评估整体的代价。
So we measure what the loss is, but we also measure the complexity, and now that all gets taken into account when we consider the overall cost.

5234
08:13:38,670 --> 08:13:47,744
没错，如果某些东西能更好地预测训练数据，它的损失可能会更低，但如果它过于复杂，它仍然可能不是最佳选择。
That yes, something might have less loss if it better predicts the training data, but if it's much more complex, it still might not be the best option that we have.

5235
08:13:48,014 --> 08:13:51,744
我们需要在损失和复杂度之间找到某种平衡。
And we need to come up with some balance between loss and complexity.

5236
08:13:51,918 --> 08:14:00,772
因此，你经常会看到这种表达方式：将复杂度乘以一个我们需要选择的参数，在这种情况下是参数 lambda，它的意思是：
And for that reason, you'll often see this represented as multiplying the complexity by some parameter that we have to choose, parameter lambda in this case, where we're saying,

5237
08:14:01,344 --> 08:14:06,544
如果 lambda 的值更大，那我们就真的想惩罚那些更复杂的假设。
if lambda is a greater value, then we really want to penalize more complex hypotheses.

5238
08:14:06,798 --> 08:14:11,104
而如果 lambda 较小，我们就会稍微少一点地惩罚复杂假设。
Whereas if lambda is smaller, we're going to penalize more complex hypotheses a little bit.

5239
08:14:11,342 --> 08:14:18,562
至于要把 lambda 的值设为多少，这取决于机器学习程序员的决定，即我想要在多大程度上
And it's up to the machine learning programmer to decide where they want to set that value of lambda for how much do I want to

5240
08:14:18,624 --> 08:14:23,024
去惩罚那些虽然能稍微更好地拟合数据、但更复杂的假设。
penalize a more complex hypothesis that might fit the data a little better.

5241
08:14:23,342 --> 08:14:25,664
再次强调，这些问题中很多都没有唯一的标准答案。
And again, there's no one right answer to a lot of these things.

5242
08:14:25,824 --> 08:14:32,784
取决于数据集，取决于你拥有的可用数据以及你试图解决的问题，你对这些参数的选择可能会有所不同。
That depending on the data set, depending on the data you have available to you and the problem you're trying to solve, your choice of these parameters may vary,

5243
08:14:32,910 --> 08:14:37,892
你可能需要做一些实验来确定最终正确的选择是什么。
and you may need to experiment a little bit to figure out what the right choice of that is ultimately going to be.

5244
08:14:38,638 --> 08:14:45,604
这种不仅考虑损失，还考虑复杂度度量的过程被称为正则化。
This process then of considering not only loss, but also some measure of the complexity is known as regularization.

5245
08:14:45,904 --> 08:14:55,744
正则化是惩罚更复杂的假设的过程，目的是为了青睐那些更有可能良好泛化的简单假设。
Regularization is the process of penalizing a hypothesis that is more complex in order to favor a simpler hypothesis that is more likely to generalize well,

5246
08:14:55,918 --> 08:15:04,144
即更有可能适用于处理其他输入点的其他情况，而这些点并非我们之前一定见过的。
more likely to be able to apply to other situations that are dealing with other input points unlike the ones that we've necessarily seen before.

5247
08:15:04,494 --> 08:15:12,932
因此，为了避免过拟合问题，你经常会看到我们在试图最小化的目标中加入一些正则化项。
So oftentimes you'll see us add some regularizing term to what we're trying to minimize in order to avoid this problem of overfitting.

5248
08:15:14,094 --> 08:15:23,396
另外一种确保不发生过拟合的方法是进行一些实验，看看我们创建的模型是否能够
Now, another way of making sure we don't overfit is to run some experiments and to see whether or not we are able to generalize our model that we've created

5249
08:15:23,776 --> 08:15:25,776
也泛用到其他数据集上。
to other data sets as well.

5250
08:15:26,126 --> 08:15:34,212
这就是为什么在做机器学习实验时，当你有一些数据并想尝试建立一个函数来预测给定输入时的结果时……
And it's for that reason that oftentimes when you're doing a machine learning experiment, when you've got some data and you want to try and come up with some function that predicts, given some input,

5251
08:15:34,414 --> 08:15:43,856
关于输出结果，你并不一定要用所有可用的数据来训练，你可以采用一种被称为留出交叉验证的方法
what the output is going to be, you don't necessarily want to do your training on all of the data you have available to you, that you could employ a method known as holdout

5252
08:15:44,016 --> 08:15:44,964
。
cross-validation.

5253
08:15:45,326 --> 08:15:48,256
在留出交叉验证中，我们会拆分数据。
Where in holdout cross-validation, we split up our data.

5254
08:15:48,416 --> 08:15:52,964
我们将数据分成训练集和测试集。
We split up our data into a training set and a testing set.

5255
08:15:53,518 --> 08:15:57,156
训练集是我们用来训练机器学习模型的那些数据。
The training set is the set of data that we're going to use to train our machine learning model.

5256
08:15:57,742 --> 08:16:05,604
而测试集是用来测试我们的机器学习模型实际表现如何的一组数据。
And the testing set is the set of data that we're going to use in order to test to see how well our machine learning model actually performed.

5257
08:16:06,510 --> 08:16:08,388
所以，学习过程是在训练集上进行的。
So the learning happens on the training set.

5258
08:16:08,656 --> 08:16:12,132
我们确定参数应该是多少，确定正确的模型是什么。
We figure out what the parameters should be, we figure out what the right model is.

5259
08:16:12,526 --> 08:16:21,776
然后我们看看，既然模型已经训练好了，它在测试集——也就是我们之前没见过的一组数据上预测的效果如何。
And then we see, all right, now that we've trained the model, see how well it does at predicting things inside of the testing set, some set of data that we haven't seen before.

5260
08:16:22,126 --> 08:16:30,736
我们的希望是，如果我们能基于现有的训练数据进行泛化，那么就能很好地预测测试集。
And the hope then is that we're going to be able to predict the testing set pretty well if we're able to generalize based on the training data that's available to us.

5261
08:16:30,926 --> 08:16:36,132
但是，如果我们对训练数据过拟合了，并且无法泛化，那么当我们查看测试集时，
If we've overfit the training data though, and we're not able to generalize, well then when we look at the testing set,

5262
08:16:36,302 --> 08:16:41,060
很可能在测试集上的预测效果就不会那么好。
it's likely going to be the case that we're not going to predict things in the testing set nearly as effectively.

5263
08:16:41,806 --> 08:16:49,380
所以这是交叉验证的一种方法，通过验证来确保我们所做的工作确实能泛用到其他数据集中。
So this is one method of cross-validation, validating to make sure that the work we have done is actually going to generalize to other data sets as well.

5264
08:16:49,646 --> 08:16:52,256
我们还可以使用其他的统计技术。
And there are other statistical techniques we can use as well.

5265
08:16:52,526 --> 08:17:01,456
这种单纯的留出交叉验证的一个缺点是，如果你只是简单地平分数据，用 50% 的数据训练，用另外 50% 的数据测试，
One of the downsides of this just holdout cross-validation is if you say I just split it 50-50, I train using 50% of the data and test using the other 50%,

5266
08:17:01,776 --> 08:17:11,652
或者你也可以选择其他的比例，那么就会有一大部分数据没被用于训练，而这些数据本可能让我们得到更好的模型。
or you could choose other percentages as well, is that there is a fair amount of data that I am now not using to train, that I might be able to get a better model as a result, for example.

5267
08:17:12,526 --> 08:17:16,004
所以另一种方法被称为 K折交叉验证。
So one approach is known as k-fold cross-validation.

5268
08:17:16,398 --> 08:17:24,816
在 K折交叉验证中，我们不是简单地把东西分成两组并运行一次实验，而是将其分成 K 个不同的集合。
In k-fold cross-validation, rather than just divide things into two sets and run one experiment, we divide things into k different sets.

5269
08:17:24,910 --> 08:17:29,444
比如我把数据分成 10 个不同的集合，然后进行 10 次不同的实验。
So maybe I divide things up into ten different sets and then run 10 different experiments.

5270
